      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner needs to recall the relationship between net force, mass, and acceleration, and the direction of the resulting motion. A single focused diagnostic question will reveal whether they understand the directionality and proportionality before they attempt a full statement.",
      "content": "If a net force pushes an object to the right, which way does the object accelerate? Right, left, or somewhere else?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "When a net force acts on an object, the object will accelerate opposite to the direction of the force, and the acceleration is directly proportional to the mass and inversely proportional to the force."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has swapped the direction (acceleration opposite the force) and inverted the proportionality (mass ↔ force). This is a classic sign‑direction error and a reversal of the proportionality relationship. I will point out each mistake, ask a confirming question, and then guide them to the correct wording. Finally I will give the full answer with a formula breakdown and exam tip.",
      "content": "I see two points where the statement got flipped:\n\n1. **Direction** – Newton's Second Law says the acceleration is *in the same direction* as the net force, not opposite. Think of pushing a sled forward; it moves forward, not backward.\n2. **Proportionality** – The acceleration grows when the force gets larger (directly proportional to **force**) and gets smaller when the mass gets larger (inversely proportional to **mass**). It’s the opposite of what you wrote.\n\nLet’s correct each part step‑by‑step. First, can you restate the direction relationship in your own words?\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\mathbf{F}_{\\text{net}} = m\\mathbf{a}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\mathbf{F}_{\\text{net}}$: the total (resultant) force acting on the object, pointing in the direction the force is applied.\n  * $m$: the mass of the object – a measure of how much matter it contains.\n  * $\\mathbf{a}$: the acceleration of the object, which points **the same way** as $\\mathbf{F}_{\\text{net}}$.\n* **Why we use it:** It tells us that a bigger push (larger force) makes the object speed up more, while a heavier object (larger mass) resists that change. The law links cause (force) to effect (acceleration) in a clear, quantitative way.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** In DBE exams, always include the direction (\"in the direction of the net force\") and the correct proportionality wording (\"directly proportional to the force and inversely proportional to the mass\"). Missing either part can cost you a mark for the definition.\n\nWhen you’re ready, write the full statement again using the corrected ideas."
    }
